      "\"It's estimating maximum temperature increase under worst-case scenarios. It's not like you're actually heating the tissue 2.3 degrees.\"",
      "\"Close enough though.\"",
      "\"Not really. It's a conservative estimate. But the principle is right: we don't want to heat tissue unnecessarily.\"",
      "I turn down the power. The TI drops to 1.8. The image quality doesn't noticeably change.",
      "\"That's better,\" Kessler says.",
      "I'm starting to understand that the power settings on the machine are probably set higher than they need to be. I could spend the afternoon turning down every transducer's power to the minimum necessary for imaging, and it would be a good use of time. But instead, I'm just making a mental note that the default isn't gospel.",
      "The Mechanical Index is displaying 0.9. This is below 1.0, which is the threshold where cavitation risk becomes significant. Cavitation is when the pressure waves in the ultrasound beam create bubbles in the tissue, and those bubbles can collapse violently and damage cells.",
      "\"Is cavitation something I should worry about?\" I ask.",
      "\"Not with standard B-mode imaging. With contrast agents, with Doppler, with higher intensity settings—then yes, it becomes a concern.\"",
      "\"So for normal scanning?\"",
      "\"ALARA applies, but cavitation isn't your main worry. Thermal effects are more relevant for standard B-mode.\"",
      "I'm writing this distinction down, as if I'm going to be tested specifically on when thermal effects dominate vs. when mechanical effects dominate. In reality, I just want to understand the machine I'm using well enough that I don't accidentally hurt someone.",
      "The break room has acquired new items in the refrigerator: two mysterious containers with no labels, a half-full bottle of something orange, and a yogurt with another aggressive Post-it note. The aggressive Post-it yogurt is still there, still uneaten. I'm starting to wonder who writes these notes and whether they're actually protecting their yogurt or just enjoying the ritual of sending hostile messages to unnamed colleagues.",
      "In the afternoon, Kessler is teaching me about obstetric imaging safety. This is serious business. First-trimester imaging is the most conservative—lowest power, shortest scanning times, clinical justification required. By second and third trimester, the imaging is less restrictive, but caution is still applied.",
      "\"You never scan longer than you need to,\" she says. \"You come in with a plan. You know what you're measuring. You measure it and you're done.\"",
      "\"What if you can't get good images?\"",
      "\"You try a different approach. A different position, a different transducer. If you still can't get good images, maybe you accept that this patient is difficult and move on. You don't scan for 20 minutes trying to optimize what you can't optimize.\"",
